(0) This is an easy exam and most people score between 45 and 51.

(1) The setup divides the class into a certain number of equivalence classe=
s and it is best to be in touch with one's equivalence class on DISCORD, so=
 that one does not make careless mistakes.

(2) This is the third time one is seeing the same material in a different l=
anguage. The first is the universe of Sets with various operations. The sec=
ond is propositional calculus. The third is Boolean Algebras which is close=
st to ordinary algebra that one learns in High School, except that Boolean =
Algebras are simpler because there are no multiples (x =3D x+x =3D x +x+ x =
and so on) and no powers (x =3Dxx=3Dxxx and so on). There are also some new=
 rules that make things a bit odd but easy, for example:

x + xy =3D x and x(x+y) =3D x

(3) So all the calculations in Sets can also be done in PC and they can als=
o be done in BA. BA gives us the simplest way to compute terms and it is ev=
en easier than truth-tables.

(3c) From now on, when faced with a problem in Sets or PC, one can always c=
ompute in BA and get one's result. One may cheat in this manner to one's he=
art's content and one is invited to do so in Problem (3c). Once one has the=
 computation in BA, one then needs to translate it symbol by symbol into th=
e language of Sets of PC as need be.

(4) Another important matter that one learns in this Exam is that the word =
'simple' does NOT mean 'what the teacher wants' and in fact must be given a=
 proper definition. It is closely related to the idea of a normal form or a=
 canonical form and requires 'equivalence classes' and an 'ordering' in the=
 background. 'To simplify a term' is to find some (minimal) canonical form =
equivalent to the term under some equivalence relation in a suitable orderi=
ng.

