<td class=3D"maincell" style=3D"font-family:sans-serif; font-size:14px; ver=
tical-align:top; box-sizing:border-box; padding:20px;">
<p>(0) This is an easy exam and most people score between 45 and 51.</p>
<p>(1) The setup divides the class into a certain number of equivalence cla=
sses and it is best to be in touch with one's equivalence class on DISCORD,=
 so that one does not make careless mistakes.&nbsp;</p>
<p>(2) This is the third time one is seeing the same material in a differen=
t language. The first is the universe of Sets with various operations. The =
second is propositional calculus. The third is Boolean Algebras which is cl=
osest to ordinary algebra that one
 learns in High School, except that Boolean Algebras are simpler because th=
ere are no multiples (x =3D x+x =3D x +x+ x and so on) and no powers (x =3D=
xx=3Dxxx and so on). There are also some new rules that make things a bit o=
dd but easy, for example:</p>
<p>x + xy =3D x and x(x+y) =3D x</p>
<p>(3) So all the calculations in Sets can also be done in PC and they can =
also be done in BA. BA gives us the simplest way to compute terms and it is=
 even easier than truth-tables.&nbsp;</p>
<p>(3c) From now on, when faced with a problem in Sets or PC, one can alway=
s compute in BA and get one's result. One may cheat in this manner to one's=
 heart's content and one is invited to do so in Problem (3c). Once one has =
the computation in BA, one then
 needs to translate it symbol by symbol into the language of Sets of PC as =
need be.&nbsp;</p>
<p>(4) Another important matter that one learns in this Exam is that the wo=
rd 'simple' does NOT mean 'what the teacher wants' and in fact must be give=
n a proper definition. It is closely related to the idea of a normal form o=
r a canonical form and requires<span>&nbsp;</span><span>'equivalence
 classes'&nbsp;</span><span>and</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span>an 'ordering=
' in the background. 'To simplify a term' is to find some (minimal) canonic=
al form equivalent to the term under some equivalence relation in a suitabl=
e ordering.&nbsp;</span></p>
</td>
